<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Sikkim</h1><p class="st-meta">1642 CE – 1975 CE · capital Gangtok</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Buddhist Himalayan kingdom ruled by the Namgyal Chogyals for over three centuries until it became a state of India in 1975.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1642 three Tibetan lamas consecrated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phuntsog-namgyal">Phuntsog Namgyal</a> as the first Chogyal at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuksom">Yuksom</a>, founding a Buddhist monarchy over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhutia">Bhutia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lepcha">Lepcha</a> peoples. Later capitals included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabdentse">Rabdentse</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tumlong">Tumlong</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Chogyals ruled through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhutia">Bhutia</a> nobles and monasteries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pemayangtse-monastery">Pemayangtse</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a> as state religion. Sikkim lost territory to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Nepal</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zhabdrung-bhutan">Bhutan</a>, and in 1861 a treaty with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> made it a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>; the capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gangtok">Gangtok</a> in 1894. Nepali settlers came to form a majority of the population.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After 1947 Sikkim became an Indian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>. Agitation against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palden-thondup-namgyal">Palden Thondup Namgyal</a> in the 1970s led to a 1975 referendum that abolished the monarchy, and Sikkim became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India's</a> 22nd state.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-sikkim.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Sikkim" width="320" height="317"><figcaption>Image: FelipeRev · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingdom_of_Sikkim.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1642 CE – 1975 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Gangtok</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Phuntsog Namgyal, Thutob Namgyal, Palden Thondup Namgyal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Vajrayana Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sikkimese, Lepcha, Nepali</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sikkim, Denzong</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1808&amp;focus=kingdom-of-sikkim" aria-label="Kingdom of Sikkim on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7004 1168 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Sikkim lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7324 1424L7364 1602M7324 1424L7018 1386M7324 1424L7018 1386"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7364 1602h0M7018 1386h0M7018 1386h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7324 1424h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7324 1424h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1808&amp;focus=kingdom-of-sikkim">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
